and the 2nd lowest two-high safety rate in the league.","offense":"The Ravens offense was about average by EPA per play, and it was much better running the ball than passing it. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Schematically, they stood out for the highest 22 personnel usage and the fewest receivers on the field in the league. Lamar Jackson ran the passing game, with Mark Andrews leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Ravens defense was about average by EPA allowed per play. It was much better against the run than the pass, ranking 8th and 20th. Tyus Bowser was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Justin Houston, who had 9.5 sacks despite playing only 44% of the snaps. In terms of scheme, they had the 2nd lowest two-high safety rate in the league.","special":"The Ravens were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 86% of field goals, which was about average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into tight ends, about 10% of the money on the books. Their defensive line was among the oldest in the league.","players":"Lamar Jackson played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 2,242 yards. Mark Andrews was the clear top target with 24% of the looks and 847 receiving yards. The backfield was a committee led by Kenyan Drake and J.K. Dobbins. On defense, Justin Houston led the team with 9.5 sacks and Marcus Williams picked off 4 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2023 and 2022 Falcons. Their defense looked most like the 2021 Lions under Aaron Glenn and the 2018 Giants.","trends":"The offense went from 19th in EPA per play in 2021 to 15th in 2022, a modest improvement. The defense moved from 27th in EPA allowed per play in 2021 to 15th in 2022, a big step forward. The biggest change in approach was number of tight ends on the field, which went from 1.2 to 1.65.","notes":"2022 is the first season with every data source available, including FTN's play design charting. It is also the last season that used the older formation labels, so singleback and I formation rates still appear here."}}